<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="diagnostics-verification" title="AW4xxx - verification and engine faults">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        Five codes for what goes wrong turning an accepted plan into bytes. Two trace back to what
        you declared; the rest point outside your source — at an artefact, a directory, or the
        engine itself. All five are errors.
    </p>

    <table style="header-row">
        <tr>
            <td>Code</td>
            <td>What happened</td>
            <td>What to do</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW4001</code></td>
            <td>Refused verifying the class</td>
            <td>Check the message; the cause is below</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW4002</code></td>
            <td>Never reported</td>
            <td>Nothing — it reserves a number</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW4003</code></td>
            <td>Refused writing the class</td>
            <td>Remove injection points from that method</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW4004</code></td>
            <td>Refused writing or verifying it</td>
            <td>Check the message; the cause is below</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW4090</code></td>
            <td>Reported wherever weaving fails</td>
            <td>Check the message; the sites are below</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <note>
        <p>
            Under Maven a refusal by the verifier arrives as <code>AW4090</code>. The plugin takes
            no verification parameter, so the fatal default stands, and the message is the
            verifier's own. The agent and the weaving class loader instead read
            <code>aether.weaver.verification</code>, and under <code>report</code> the refused
            class is kept rather than thrown. The agent still prints nothing for it once installed:
            it drains its diagnostics only once, while installing.
            <a href="configuration-keys.topic">Configuration keys</a> has the key;
            <a href="diagnostics.topic">Diagnostic codes</a> has the drain.
        </p>
    </note>

    <chapter title="What is yours to fix" id="yours">

        <p>Some of these have a cause in your own weave. Rule both out before you open an issue.</p>

        <list>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A <code>@Redirect</code> handler may declare a trailing <code>Callback</code>,
                    and the engine accepts it — nothing pushes one, so the call it writes is a
                    parameter short. Where the redirected value is discarded, the writer refuses
                    the bytes first: <code>AW4004</code>. Where it feeds a larger expression,
                    other values sit beneath it on the stack, so the class is written but fails
                    verification: <code>AW4001</code>. Remove the parameter either way; a redirect
                    stands in for the operation, so there is nothing to cancel.
                    <a href="handlers-and-callbacks.topic">Handlers and callbacks</a> has the bands
                    a handler is read in.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <code>AW4003</code> is the class file's own limit: 65535 bytes of code in one
                    method. Only the call costs bytes there, not the handler's body. Remove
                    injection points from that method; a smaller handler buys nothing.
                    <a href="redirect-and-wrap.topic">Redirect and Wrap</a> replace an operation
                    instead of adding to one.
                </p>
            </li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Faults with no code of their own" id="internal">

        <p>
            <code>AW4090</code> is what a driver reports when nothing more specific fits. Every
            driver reports it against whatever it was working on; what the message names
            differs by condition below.
        </p>

        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="An exception escaped while weaving a class">
                <p>
                    <a href="diagnostics.topic">Diagnostic codes</a> has what each driver's error
                    policy does with it. Whatever it decides, no dump exists for these bytes — the
                    write that would produce one never runs. Under Maven only a verifier's
                    <code>WeaveException</code> becomes <code>AW4090</code> this way; any other
                    exception aborts the goal instead, unreported. Give the class name and the
                    message to the <a href="%issues%">issue tracker</a>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The verifier refused the woven bytes and threw">
                <p>
                    Use the message it carries: it is the <code>AW4001</code> or the
                    <code>AW4004</code> above, and the remedy there is the one to follow.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A class dump could not be written">
                <p>
                    The woven class is written all the same. Check that the dump directory exists
                    and is writable, or unset it.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="No injector is registered for a declaration's kind">
                <p>
                    That declaration is skipped and the rest of the class is woven. Add the plugin
                    that contributes the kind. <a href="plugins.topic">Extending the engine</a>
                    covers how one is loaded.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A dependency jar could not be read">
                <p>
                    Weaving that artefact stops where the read failed. Check the artefact, then run
                    the build again.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What a report needs" id="report">

        <p>Two things make an engine fault reproducible.</p>

        <list>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The whole line: the code, the message, and every indented detail under it.
                    <a href="diagnostics.topic">Diagnostic codes</a> reads one apart. A class dump
                    will not add to it — none of the five codes above leaves one behind.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The weave that was applied, and the target's class file where you may share it.
                </p>
            </li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

